A very simple PHP example that sends an HTTP POST request to a remote site

$url = "http://www.example.com/tester.phtml";
$post_data = array('postvar1' => 'value1');

$ch = curl_init($url);
// return the response instead of sending it to stdout:
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true);
// set the POST data, corresponding method and headers:
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, http_build_query($post_data));
// send the request and get the response
$server_output = curl_exec($ch);

URLEncoder is the way to go. You only need to keep in mind to encode only the individual query string parameter name and/or value, not the entire URL, for sure not the query string parameter separator character & nor the parameter name-value separator character =.

String q = "random word £500 bank $";
String url = "https://example.com?q=" + URLEncoder.encode(q, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

When you're still not on Java 10 or newer, then use StandardCharsets.UTF_8.name() as charset argument, or when you're still not on Java 7 or newer, then use "UTF-8".

Note that spaces in query parameters are represented by +, not %20, which is legitimately valid. The %20 is usually to be used to represent spaces in URI itself (the part before the URI-query string separator character ?), not in query string

